    "question": {
      ":question/id": "20558f5d-bdee-55b5-9388-5a75c1263d7e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{7}{2})^{2}-1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (3,4]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{9}{2})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (4,5]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{7}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(3,4]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{13}{4}$ and $\\frac{15}{4}$, both with output $- \\frac{15}{16}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{9}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{7}{2}\\ge \\frac{7}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(4,5]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $4\\ge \\frac{7}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
