    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9a250ab5-6bc3-5006-8576-f28c184dfbb9",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}-4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [\\frac{1}{3},1)$\n- **III.** $x\\in [- \\frac{2}{3},0)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(0,\\frac{2}{3}]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$, both with output $- \\frac{143}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[\\frac{1}{3},1)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{3}\\ge \\frac{1}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[- \\frac{2}{3},0)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $0\\le \\frac{1}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
