    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a14fc308-0ae5-5bf0-bfa1-acd24b900d06",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{11}{4})^{2}+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [- \\frac{11}{4},- \\frac{9}{4})$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{5}{2},-2]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-3,- \\frac{5}{2}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{11}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[- \\frac{11}{4},- \\frac{9}{4})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{4}\\ge - \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{5}{2},-2]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{5}{2}\\ge - \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-3,- \\frac{5}{2}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{23}{8}$ and $- \\frac{21}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{193}{64}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b9abed84-9ff9-5043-93c7-c44115d467f3",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
