    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "968d6f22-b0c0-5611-bca8-feaa8d3d8559",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{3})^{2}-3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{2}{3},0]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (- \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{1}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{2}{3},0]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $- \\frac{1}{6}$, both with output $- \\frac{107}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{2}{3}\\le - \\frac{1}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(- \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{1}{3}\\ge - \\frac{1}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "712bd636-2edd-52ff-9051-9e9cecd15310",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
