    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bc7a1fb0-1204-560b-b67b-02a379a2d97d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-\\frac{3}{2}|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [0,1)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (1,2]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{3}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[0,1)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $1\\le \\frac{3}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(1,2]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{5}{4}$ and $\\frac{7}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{3}{2}\\ge \\frac{3}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
