    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4052e152-790c-54ef-bc5b-777ee9c85441",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x+\\frac{7}{2}|-4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-4,-3]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{7}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{7}{2}\\le - \\frac{7}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{7}{2}\\le - \\frac{7}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-4,-3]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{15}{4}$ and $- \\frac{13}{4}$, both with output $- \\frac{15}{4}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "215d6d13-bf41-515e-81d4-6c33bf47b521",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
