    "question": {
      ":question/id": "024d7afb-33fd-5294-98e8-3b1544cbd85a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{4}{3})^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,- \\frac{1}{3}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (- \\frac{5}{3},-1]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{4}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{4}{3}\\ge - \\frac{4}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-1,- \\frac{1}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-1\\ge - \\frac{4}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(- \\frac{5}{3},-1]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{3}{2}$ and $- \\frac{7}{6}$, both with output $- \\frac{71}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
