    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c61c8d62-b135-5b45-bca6-07bc7d7c20aa",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-\\frac{2}{3}|,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [0,\\frac{2}{3})$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{1}{3},1]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{2}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[0,\\frac{2}{3})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{2}{3}\\le \\frac{2}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{1}{3},1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{6}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{6}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(0,\\frac{2}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{2}{3}\\le \\frac{2}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
