      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{11}{3})^{2}-1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [- \\frac{11}{3},-3)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{11}{3},-3]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-4,- \\frac{10}{3}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{11}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[- \\frac{11}{3},-3)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{3}\\ge - \\frac{11}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{11}{3},-3]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{3}\\ge - \\frac{11}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-4,- \\frac{10}{3}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{23}{6}$ and $- \\frac{7}{2}$, both with output $- \\frac{35}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
