    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d324c31d-4cfa-58c7-ac49-b6667a90bf6f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{10}{3})^{2}+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (3,\\frac{11}{3}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{10}{3},4]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [\\frac{10}{3},4)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{10}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(3,\\frac{11}{3}]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{19}{6}$ and $\\frac{7}{2}$, both with output $\\frac{145}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{10}{3},4]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{10}{3}\\ge \\frac{10}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[\\frac{10}{3},4)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{10}{3}\\ge \\frac{10}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "99dc3e47-7312-5e80-af90-300e10b3c355",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
