    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2f715301-8d2b-593a-83c4-884df2efbbb6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-\\frac{15}{4}|,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (\\frac{15}{4},\\frac{17}{4}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [3,\\frac{7}{2})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (\\frac{7}{2},4]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{15}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(\\frac{15}{4},\\frac{17}{4}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{15}{4}\\ge \\frac{15}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[3,\\frac{7}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{7}{2}\\le \\frac{15}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(\\frac{7}{2},4]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{29}{8}$ and $\\frac{31}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{8}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
