    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e12c8458-2748-5579-b93a-04eee47e1332",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{3}{4})^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{1}{2},0]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,- \\frac{1}{2}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [- \\frac{3}{2},-1)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{3}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{1}{2},0]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{1}{2}\\ge - \\frac{3}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-1,- \\frac{1}{2}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{7}{8}$ and $- \\frac{5}{8}$, both with output $- \\frac{127}{64}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[- \\frac{3}{2},-1)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-1\\le - \\frac{3}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
