    "question": {
      ":question/id": "39c460af-5613-5cc0-ab2f-96df885de2ce",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+\\frac{2}{3})^{2}+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-1,- \\frac{1}{3}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{2}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{1}{3}\\ge - \\frac{2}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{4}{3},- \\frac{2}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{2}{3}\\le - \\frac{2}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-1,- \\frac{1}{3}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{5}{6}$ and $- \\frac{1}{2}$, both with output $\\frac{145}{36}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2efdf5b6-6ec2-56a8-a324-13d8fe6df5d4",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
