      ":question/id": "d5f17cee-abc8-5728-a976-be9427a257b6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x+\\frac{11}{3}|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-4,- \\frac{10}{3}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{13}{3},- \\frac{11}{3}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [- \\frac{11}{3},-3)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{11}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(-4,- \\frac{10}{3}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{23}{6}$ and $- \\frac{7}{2}$, both with output $\\frac{13}{6}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{13}{3},- \\frac{11}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{3}\\le - \\frac{11}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[- \\frac{11}{3},-3)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{3}\\ge - \\frac{11}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
