      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x+\\frac{1}{2}|+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,0]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [-2,-1)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{1}{2}\\le - \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-1,0]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{3}{4}$ and $- \\frac{1}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{17}{4}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[-2,-1)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-1\\le - \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
