    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4136d4f4-5c21-594e-994c-a5b983944ffb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x+\\frac{9}{4}|+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (- \\frac{5}{2},-2]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [-3,- \\frac{5}{2})$\n- **III.** $x\\in [- \\frac{9}{4},- \\frac{7}{4})$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{9}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(- \\frac{5}{2},-2]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{19}{8}$ and $- \\frac{17}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{25}{8}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[-3,- \\frac{5}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{5}{2}\\le - \\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[- \\frac{9}{4},- \\frac{7}{4})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{9}{4}\\ge - \\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
