      ":question/id": "5f9d7b1b-602b-588d-9e0e-55d6604dab21",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-\\frac{11}{4}|-1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (\\frac{5}{2},3]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [2,\\frac{5}{2})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (\\frac{11}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{11}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(\\frac{5}{2},3]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{21}{8}$ and $\\frac{23}{8}$, both with output $- \\frac{7}{8}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[2,\\frac{5}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(\\frac{11}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{11}{4}\\ge \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
